* {{audio|en|en-us-certain.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɜː(ɹ)tən|s=2}}
* {{hyphenation|en|cer|tain}}

===Adjective===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-adj|more|er}}

# [[sure|Sure]] in one's mind, [[positive]]; absolutely [[confident]] in the truth of something.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:certain}}
#: {{ux|en|I was '''certain''' of my decision.}}
#* {{RQ:Marryat Peter Simple|volume=III|chapter=VIII|page=113|passage={{...}} I think, nay, I may say that I'm '''sartain''', we'll have a hurricane afore morning. It's not the first time I've cruised in these latitudes.}}
# Not to be [[doubted]] or [[denied]]; [[established]] as a [[fact]].
#: {{ux|en|Now that more experiments have been run, the theory is '''certain''' and the argument is settled.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Daniel|2|45|page=944|column=1||{{...}}the dreame ''is'' '''certaine''', and the interpretation thereof ſure.}}
# Sure to [[happen]], [[inevitable]]; [[assured]].
#: {{ux|en|It is '''certain''' that Spain will reach the finals. / Spain is now '''certain''' to reach the finals. / Spain is now '''certain''' of a place in the finals.}}
#: {{ux|en|Bankruptcy is the '''certain''' outcome of your constant gambling and lending.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2 Q1|III|ii|page=49|passage={{...}}death (as the Pſalmiſt ſaith) is '''certaine''' to all, all ſhall die.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Aureng-zebe|29|passage=How vain is Virtue which directs our ways <br> Through '''certain''' danger to uncertain praiſe!}}
# Unfailing; [[infallible]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1702|author=w:Richard Mead|title=Mechanical Account of Poisons|passage=I have often wished, that I knew so '''certain''' a remedy in any other disease}}
# [[fixed|Fixed]]; [[regular]]; [[determinate]].
#: {{coi|en|at '''certain''' intervals}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Exodus|16|4|page=153|column=1|passage=Then ſaid the {{smc|Lord}} vnto Moſes, Behold, I will raine bread from heauen for you: and the people ſhall goe out, and gather a '''certaine''' rate euery day, that I may proue them, whether they will walke in my Law, or no.}}
# [[particular|Particular]] and [[definite]], but [[unspecified]] or [[unnamed]]; {{n-g|used to introduce someone or something without going into further detail}}.
#: ''Every wine has a '''certain''' distinctive character which sets it apart from all others.''
#: ''Each morning, she would see a '''certain''' man rush past her window on his way to work.''
#* {{RQ:KJV|Luke|5|12|page=1287|column=1|passage=And it came to paſſe, when he was in a '''certaine''' citie{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay Goldsmith|365|passage=About everything that he wrote, serious or sportive, there was a '''certain''' natural grace and decorum{{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|preceded by "a"|of a person}} [[named|Named]] but not previously [[mention]]ed.
#: {{syn|en|one}}
#: ''Looking inside the cover, they learned that the book had once belonged to a '''certain''' R. Jones.''
# {{lb|en|preceded by "a"|of a person}} Used before the name of someone famous that people are expected to know.
#: {{syn|en|one}}
#: ''Since the last British government to make such a proposal was that of a '''certain''' Margaret Thatcher, it might not seem unreasonable.''
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Determined; resolved.
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=VIII|page=256|lines=952–953|passage=However I with thee have fixt my Lot, <br> '''Certain''' to undergoe like doom{{...}}|year=1873}}

